  "summary": "Indian vitreous enamel: jewel colors fused into gold and silver, red the crown of Jaipur work.",
  "description": "Meenakari fuses ground glass onto gold, silver or copper in kiln-fired layers — champlevé and cloisonné cells filled with green, blue, white and above all the deep pigeon-blood red that Jaipur made famous. Brought to maturity when Raja Man Singh brought Persian-trained enamelers to Jaipur in the 1500s, the art enameled sword hilts, ritual thalis, huqqa bases and above all the invisible backs of kundan-set jewelry, where hidden meena glows against the skin. Jaipur's Maniharon ka Rasta lanes still ring with enamel kilns; Lucknow and Varanasi keep blue-work schools.",
